# 3. NON-NEGOTIABLE SAFETY RULES

Before implementation, follow these rules.

## A. Preserve the production Owner application

DO NOT:

- Delete the existing Manager Portal.
- Remove the existing Manager routes from the root application.
- Break Owner authentication.
- Break Owner dashboard functionality.
- Modify existing Owner business logic unnecessarily.
- Change production Firestore collections.
- Rename existing Firestore fields.
- Migrate existing business documents.
- Remove existing authentication mechanisms.
- Change production Firebase project configuration.
- Modify production Firestore rules without explicit approval.
- Deploy anything automatically.

The existing root Manager implementation must remain available as a fallback during Phase 4.

## B. No destructive changes

Do not delete or overwrite existing functionality merely because it appears redundant.

Do not perform:

- Firestore deletions.
- Data migrations.
- Collection renaming.
- Authentication user deletion.
- Branch reassignment.
- Production credential changes.
- Production deployments.

## C. No security shortcuts

Never:

- Trust a branch ID supplied by the browser without server authorization.
- Trust localStorage for authorization.
- Trust frontend filtering as the only access-control mechanism.
- Expose Firebase Admin credentials in frontend code.
- Store manager PINs in plaintext.
- Bypass Firestore security rules.
- Replace custom-token authentication with insecure authentication.
- Allow managers to access arbitrary businesses.
- Give managers Owner permissions.

## D. Never claim tests passed without executing them

For every test/build:

- Execute the actual command.
- Record the actual result.
- Identify failures.
- Never claim Firebase Emulator tests passed if they were not executed.
- Never claim a security test passed merely because the code appears correct.

